     4	      <h1 id="methodology-heading">Methodology</h1>
     5	      <p class="introduction">
     6	        Exposed makes MPs' registered interests easier to search and explore.
     7	        Here is where the data comes from, how we connect records, and what to
     8	        check before using it.
     9	      </p>
    10	    </header>
    11	
    12	    <section aria-labelledby="sources">
    13	      <h2 id="sources">Sources and updates</h2>
    14	      <p>
    15	        We use UK Parliament's <a
    16	          href="https://members-api.parliament.uk/index.html">Members API</a
    17	        >
    18	        for member profiles and current House of Commons membership, and its
    19	        <a href="https://interests-api.parliament.uk/index.html"
    20	          >Interests API</a
    21	        > for registered interests. Declarations include expired entries for MPs held
    22	        in our database. This is not a complete history of every former MP.
    23	      </p>
    24	      <p>
    25	        Read the <a
    26	          href="https://www.parliament.uk/mps-lords-and-offices/standards-and-financial-interests/parliamentary-commissioner-for-standards/registers-of-interests/register-of-members-financial-interests/"
    27	          >official Register of Members' Financial Interests</a
    28	        > for the source material.
    29	      </p>
    30	      <p>
    31	        The data is updated daily. Updates reflect what Parliament has
    32	        published; they are not live. A new declaration or correction may take
    33	        time to appear here.
    34	      </p>
    35	    </section>
    36	
    37	    <section aria-labelledby="matching">
    38	      <h2 id="matching">Matching and grouping funders</h2>
    39	      <p>
    40	        A funder can appear under several names. We connect records using
    41	        company numbers, names and addresses, and explicitly recorded
    42	        alternative names. Company numbers come from the declarations and are
    43	        not independently verified against Companies House. Conflicting company
    44	        numbers prevent us from treating records as the same legal company.
    45	      </p>
    46	      <p>
    47	        Exact donor names can match even when addresses are missing or
    48	        different. This can join different people or organisations that share a
    49	        name. A similar-looking name alone is not a confirmed match. We preserve
    50	        the original names so you can check the evidence.
    51	      </p>
    52	      <p>
    53	        Separately, we group some funders for browsing using shared recorded
    54	        names, alternative names and selected name families, such as BBC, GB
    55	        News and Premier League. A displayed group is not proof that its entries
    56	        are one legal company or share an owner.
    57	      </p>
    58	      <p>
    59	        A declaration can name a donor, a payer and an ultimate payer. These
    60	        roles can refer to different people or organisations. We use the
    61	        declaration's evidence to attribute each payment once, rather than
    62	        counting it under every named role.
    63	      </p>
    64	    </section>
    65	
    66	    <section aria-labelledby="interpretation">
    67	      <h2 id="interpretation">Reading the data</h2>
    68	      <p>
    69	        Totals keep currencies separate. Entries without an amount do not
    70	        contribute to a total; totals affected by missing amounts are marked as
    71	        partial. Party groups use each MP's latest stored party, which may
    72	        differ from their party at the time of a payment.
    73	      </p>
    74	      <p>
    75	        Check the original declarations before publishing research or drawing
    76	        conclusions. Matching and grouping can be wrong, and the source may be
    77	        incomplete or later corrected. A registered interest does not establish
